<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/049Y">Remark <span data-tag="049Y">8.1.9.2</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="049X" href="/tag/049X">8.1.9.1</a>, let $C$ be a vertex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C}$ denote the fiber $\{ C\}  \times _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$. Note that a morphism $u$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C}$ is an isomorphism if and only if it is $U$-cocartesian when viewed as a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ (Proposition <a data-tag="01UJ" href="/tag/01UJ">5.1.4.12</a>). It follows that the fiber $\{ C\}  \times _{ \operatorname{Cospan}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \operatorname{Cospan}^{\mathrm{all},W}(\operatorname{\mathcal{E}})$ can be identified with the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Cospan}^{ \mathrm{all}, \mathrm{iso}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C} )$. In particular, Proposition <a data-tag="049L" href="/tag/049L">8.1.7.6</a> supplies an equivalence of $\infty $-categories </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \rho _{+}: \{ C\}  \times _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\hookrightarrow \{ C\}  \times _{ \operatorname{Cospan}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \operatorname{Cospan}^{ \mathrm{all}, W}( \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}). \]
</div>
</article>
